Subject: [PATCH] DRM: detect input devices plugged in after startup

InitEvdevInput() only ever scans /dev/input once, at startup - a
keyboard (or any other input device) plugged in while the app is
already running was never found at all, confirmed on real hardware.

Adds RescanEvdevInputForNewDevices(), a throttled (every 2 seconds,
called from PollInputEvents()) re-scan of /dev/input that skips
devices already known (tracked via a new platform.knownEvdevPaths[]
list, populated by ConfigureEvdevDevice() for every device it accepts,
of any kind - one shared recording point rather than duplicating this
in each device-kind branch) and configures only genuinely new ones,
exactly like the startup scan does.

Deliberately a simple, throttled directory re-scan rather than a
netlink/uevent-based listener (what udev itself uses) - much less code
and much easier to reason about correctness of, and more than
responsive enough for a human plugging in a device and expecting it to
work within a couple of seconds, which is the actual real-world need
here rather than a high-frequency hotplug scenario.

Also cleans up two redundant TRACELOG calls accidentally introduced by
the preceding 0003 mouse patch (the touch and mouse accept-branches
each had their own "Initialized input device..." log in addition to
the pre-existing shared one at the end of the function, printing the
same information twice) - now deviceKindStr is set correctly in both
branches and only the shared trailing log fires, as originally
designed.

Verified: the rescan's core "is this path already known" logic tested
in isolation against 3 scenarios (initial scan configures all found
devices; a rescan with one genuinely new device alongside already-
known ones configures only the new one; a rescan with no new devices
configures nothing again) - all passing. Full file (with 0001, 0002,
0003 already applied) compiles cleanly, and the app links and starts
without crashing against the resulting library.

Upstream-Status: Pending

